WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.588 --> 00:00:03.588
(audience applauds)

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:09.483
- Graduates of the Class of 2023,

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:12.813
family members and friends,

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:18.240
it gives me great pleasure to welcome you

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:20.433
and to offer a few words this morning.

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:26.930
First, there is a wonderful Yale tradition

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:28.983
that I'd like to honor right now.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:33.330
So may I ask all of the families

00:00:33.330 --> 00:00:37.800
and friends who are here today to rise

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:40.080
and to recognize the outstanding

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:44.315
and graduating members
of the Class of 2023?

00:00:44.315 --> 00:00:48.232
(audience cheers and applauds)

00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:18.300
And now, may I ask the Class of 2023

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:21.390
to consider all those who have supported

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:24.780
your arrival at this milestone

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:27.393
and to please rise and recognize them?

00:01:28.303 --> 00:01:32.220
(audience cheers and applauds)

00:01:51.966 --> 00:01:55.590
Okay, that concludes the
aerobic exercise portion

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:59.163
of our program.
(audience chuckles)

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:05.060
Today, as we assemble at an
inflection point in your lives

00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:09.690
between the shortest,
gladdest years of life

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:13.773
and exciting new chapters
that lie beyond them,

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:17.790
I wanna discuss the importance
of community engagement

00:02:17.790 --> 00:02:20.823
here in New Haven and in your future.

00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:25.590
So raise your hand if you participated

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:28.560
in a service organization on campus

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:32.373
or in our local community
during your time at Yale.

00:02:34.056 --> 00:02:38.940
Hey, that's a lot of hands.
(audience claps)

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:41.280
It's a lot of hands, but I'm not surprised

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:44.970
because I know that nearly all
of you have immersed yourself

00:02:44.970 --> 00:02:48.930
in the work of civic betterment
in one form or another

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:50.943
over the past four years.

00:02:52.230 --> 00:02:54.630
Despite the rigors of your studies,

00:02:54.630 --> 00:02:58.260
you have promoted literacy
with New Haven Reads,

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:00.450
brought the basics of computer science

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:04.560
to public school classrooms
through Code Haven,

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:06.390
and tutored immigrants for whom English

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:10.893
is not a first language
as Bridges ESL volunteers.

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:15.180
You've likewise helped
us to alleviate housing

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:17.850
and food insecurity through the Yale

00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:21.270
Hunger and Homelessness Action Project,

00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:25.650
supported your student neighbors
as Walden Peer counselors

00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:28.740
and engaged in remedial climate action

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:31.203
with the Urban Resources Initiative.

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:36.840
Even on the heels of
final exams last week,

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:41.463
many of you volunteered in
Yale Day of Service activities.

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:48.140
You have collectively pursued
unfinished work in our society

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:51.720
and affirmed what Coretta Scott King

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:56.190
observed on this campus in 1969,

00:03:56.190 --> 00:04:01.190
that students want to start
today on the world of tomorrow.

00:04:02.850 --> 00:04:06.750
Although not necessarily
learned in the classroom,

00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:10.590
this spirit of service is every bit a part

00:04:10.590 --> 00:04:13.833
of what makes a Yale
education distinctive.

00:04:15.570 --> 00:04:19.080
And in all, we estimate
that you and your classmates

00:04:19.080 --> 00:04:24.080
contributed a remarkable
150,000 hours each year,

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:28.683
each year you were here,
to community-based causes.

00:04:30.450 --> 00:04:32.580
Rather than reflect on how your work

00:04:32.580 --> 00:04:34.920
in this community will reverberate

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:39.920
long after you exit Phelps
Gate, I want to implore you

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:44.823
to replicate it no
matter where you go next.

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:50.610
For several years now, even
before the COVID-19 pandemic,

00:04:50.610 --> 00:04:54.300
this country has seen a
decline in social capital

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:58.230
and the value we derive
from positive connections

00:04:58.230 --> 00:04:59.823
with other people.

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:05.880
Political scientist, Robert
Putnam, diagnosed this decay

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:09.450
through something that actually
used to be fairly common

00:05:09.450 --> 00:05:13.680
in my time as a teenager
in Buffalo, New York,

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:14.943
bowling leagues.

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:19.230
Putnam, and eminent Yale alumnus,

00:05:19.230 --> 00:05:21.910
observed that more Americans are bowling

00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:25.743
but fewer in organized leagues.

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:30.660
So he used the vivid
metaphor of bowling alone

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:34.380
to represent the breakdown of
traditional social networks

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:36.183
in contemporary society.

00:05:38.070 --> 00:05:41.880
As a touchstone of his thesis,
Putnam pointed to a reduction

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:45.780
in membership among
organizations like PTAs,

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:50.643
the Red Cross, Lions Clubs
from its mid-20th century peak.

00:05:52.470 --> 00:05:57.470
He found, in short, that we
are dropping out in droves

00:05:57.750 --> 00:06:00.123
from organized community life.

00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:06.930
In the two decades since
"Bowling Alone's" publication,

00:06:06.930 --> 00:06:11.610
US Census data have similarly
depicted a sharp decrease

00:06:11.610 --> 00:06:13.083
in community involvement.

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:18.150
The hours donated annually
by volunteers, for example,

00:06:18.150 --> 00:06:21.240
halved between the start of the century

00:06:21.240 --> 00:06:23.433
and your first year at Yale.

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:27.000
And the national volunteering rate

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:30.360
dropped an additional
seven percentage points

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:32.193
by the time you were a junior.

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:37.053
So what happens when social ties weaken?

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:41.643
Certainly something is
lost in our communities.

00:06:43.140 --> 00:06:46.920
But we also retreat into ourselves.

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:50.823
We become isolated, and we feel lonely.

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:56.400
Surveys indicate the alarming
prevalence of these feelings.

00:06:56.400 --> 00:07:01.400
60% of Americans, including
a full 75% of younger people

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:06.003
like yourselves now
struggle with loneliness.

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:10.890
Some weeks ago, this frayed social fabric

00:07:10.890 --> 00:07:14.340
compelled US Surgeon General Vivek Murthy,

00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:16.320
also a Yale alumnus,

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:20.343
to declare a loneliness
epidemic in America.

00:07:21.937 --> 00:07:26.520
"The ramifications for
our health and wellbeing

00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:28.443
are wide ranging," he says,

00:07:29.467 --> 00:07:32.637
"but an antidote is within reach."

00:07:33.930 --> 00:07:37.290
As Dr. Murthy remarked
last semester here at Yale,

00:07:37.290 --> 00:07:41.730
community service activities
connect us with somebody deeply

00:07:41.730 --> 00:07:45.990
on something that matters,
but also reaffirmed to us

00:07:45.990 --> 00:07:49.080
that we have value to bring to the world.

00:07:49.080 --> 00:07:52.740
They help us to rebuild that
esteem which gets shredded

00:07:52.740 --> 00:07:53.853
when we are lonely.

00:07:55.560 --> 00:08:00.560
Of course, service activities
are as solitary for society

00:08:00.570 --> 00:08:03.453
as they are for ourselves.

00:08:04.950 --> 00:08:08.310
Social capital can contribute
to positive outcomes

00:08:08.310 --> 00:08:10.830
in education, children's welfare,

00:08:10.830 --> 00:08:15.210
the safety of our neighborhoods,
economic prosperity,

00:08:15.210 --> 00:08:17.313
and even democracy itself.

00:08:18.780 --> 00:08:22.320
Our civic and social lives form the mortar

00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:26.583
of American society and a
pillar of public health.

00:08:28.140 --> 00:08:32.853
We must, therefore, redouble
our resolve to rescue them.

00:08:34.860 --> 00:08:38.520
In doing so, we should be
mindful of a distinction

00:08:38.520 --> 00:08:41.610
articulated by philosopher John Dewey,

00:08:41.610 --> 00:08:45.273
on whom Yale bestowed an
honorary degree in 1951.

00:08:46.567 --> 00:08:49.177
"Many social reformers," he lamented,

00:08:49.177 --> 00:08:52.770
"however noble their
intentions have failed to meet

00:08:52.770 --> 00:08:55.200
their ambitions because
they were committed

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:59.877
to doing good for rather
than with others."

00:09:01.020 --> 00:09:04.470
So for our part, we aspire to make Yale

00:09:04.470 --> 00:09:07.740
the most civically-engaged university

00:09:07.740 --> 00:09:10.710
by strengthening the ties that bind us

00:09:10.710 --> 00:09:13.053
to our host city, New Haven.

00:09:15.660 --> 00:09:18.813
Like most of you, I arrived
in New Haven as a visitor.

00:09:19.710 --> 00:09:22.290
I remember my first days at Yale

00:09:22.290 --> 00:09:25.173
as an entering graduate student in 1981.

00:09:26.190 --> 00:09:30.030
I was blown away by the
beauty of the stone buildings

00:09:30.030 --> 00:09:33.003
and by the tastiness of the local pizza.

00:09:34.950 --> 00:09:36.270
Over the next four decades,

00:09:36.270 --> 00:09:38.970
I discovered that New
Haven is a fascinating

00:09:38.970 --> 00:09:41.760
and vibrant community in which to live.

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:46.110
The Elm City quickly
became my and Marta's home

00:09:46.110 --> 00:09:49.233
and an integral part of our identities.

00:09:50.850 --> 00:09:54.900
Your time here may well
be shorter than ours,

00:09:54.900 --> 00:09:57.660
although I urge you to
consider adding your talents

00:09:57.660 --> 00:10:00.660
to the rich mix of what
makes this a great place

00:10:00.660 --> 00:10:02.103
to work and live.

00:10:03.480 --> 00:10:07.803
But whether you remain in New
Haven or not after graduation,

00:10:08.850 --> 00:10:13.410
we all experience a
distinct bond between Yale

00:10:13.410 --> 00:10:14.763
and our host city.

00:10:16.260 --> 00:10:17.910
And the depth of that relationship

00:10:17.910 --> 00:10:22.260
was reflected most recently
by the historic pledge

00:10:22.260 --> 00:10:26.700
to extend our lead as the
institution that makes the largest

00:10:26.700 --> 00:10:30.270
annual voluntary financial contribution

00:10:30.270 --> 00:10:34.383
of any US college or
university to its home city.

00:10:36.060 --> 00:10:39.333
But-
(audience applauds)

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:45.930
But a university's
responsibility to its host city

00:10:45.930 --> 00:10:49.740
goes beyond, goes beyond
financial contributions,

00:10:49.740 --> 00:10:53.433
what Dewey might have
deemed worthwhile ends.

00:10:54.960 --> 00:10:58.950
It should also be intellectual,
it should be innovative,

00:10:58.950 --> 00:11:03.950
it should be human, it should
be with as well as for.

00:11:06.240 --> 00:11:09.420
The metaphor of the ivory tower

00:11:09.420 --> 00:11:12.270
walled off from its surroundings,

00:11:12.270 --> 00:11:17.270
and at Yale, protected by moats
and gates is no longer apt.

00:11:18.690 --> 00:11:22.440
What is real instead is a social contract

00:11:22.440 --> 00:11:27.000
in which we willingly and
readily obligate ourselves

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:29.493
to the welfare of one another.

00:11:31.470 --> 00:11:36.240
So our task, even as we
increase our voluntary payments,

00:11:36.240 --> 00:11:39.363
is to reinforce our culture of engagement.

00:11:41.010 --> 00:11:45.930
Alumni, such as Patricia
Melton, exemplify this ideal.

00:11:45.930 --> 00:11:47.730
A native of Cleveland, Ohio,

00:11:47.730 --> 00:11:51.630
a 1983 graduate of Yale College,

00:11:51.630 --> 00:11:55.920
and with similar to about 20% of you,

00:11:55.920 --> 00:11:59.520
the first in her family to attend college,

00:11:59.520 --> 00:12:03.900
Patricia has dedicated her
career to helping young people

00:12:03.900 --> 00:12:05.343
in our community.

00:12:06.840 --> 00:12:11.550
For over a decade, she
has led New Haven Promise,

00:12:11.550 --> 00:12:14.920
a Yale-funded scholarship and
career development program

00:12:15.780 --> 00:12:18.513
that is one of our most
treasured collaborations.

00:12:19.710 --> 00:12:22.414
Patricia's inspired
work delivers the dream

00:12:22.414 --> 00:12:25.170
of college for scores of young people

00:12:25.170 --> 00:12:27.783
who otherwise wouldn't
be able to afford it.

00:12:29.190 --> 00:12:33.510
Importantly, the Promise program
encourages college students

00:12:33.510 --> 00:12:38.510
to return to New Haven following
graduation just as she did.

00:12:39.930 --> 00:12:41.703
The results have been superb.

00:12:42.750 --> 00:12:45.930
By the end of this decade, we
expect to have welcomed back

00:12:45.930 --> 00:12:49.680
thousands of promise
alumni who will accelerate

00:12:49.680 --> 00:12:52.050
the Renaissance well underway in our city,

00:12:52.050 --> 00:12:54.810
alongside many other Yale initiatives

00:12:54.810 --> 00:12:58.620
such as the recently established
Pennington Fellowship

00:12:58.620 --> 00:13:01.143
and the Center for Inclusive Growth.

00:13:02.400 --> 00:13:05.340
And we also look forward
to joining the mayor's

00:13:05.340 --> 00:13:07.830
recently announced tutoring program

00:13:07.830 --> 00:13:09.543
for New Haven School children.

00:13:11.430 --> 00:13:14.880
It is heartening to
consider these extraordinary

00:13:14.880 --> 00:13:19.860
community partnerships that
alumni like Patricia unleash,

00:13:19.860 --> 00:13:24.123
and as Yale's newest
graduates, you can soon enrich.

00:13:26.370 --> 00:13:28.023
Before we say farewell,

00:13:29.130 --> 00:13:33.333
I wish to hearken back
to when we first met.

00:13:34.290 --> 00:13:38.370
You may recall that four years
ago I penned an open letter

00:13:38.370 --> 00:13:41.523
to welcome the Class of 2023.

00:13:42.600 --> 00:13:46.383
In it, I asked you to consider
a series of questions,

00:13:47.220 --> 00:13:50.853
among them, why are we here?

00:13:52.620 --> 00:13:54.093
Why is Yale here?

00:13:55.950 --> 00:13:58.383
What is our purpose?

00:13:59.850 --> 00:14:02.130
Of course, improving the
world through research

00:14:02.130 --> 00:14:04.533
and education is Yale's mission.

00:14:05.730 --> 00:14:07.860
The crises that surround us,

00:14:07.860 --> 00:14:11.520
pressing in nature and
often planetary in scale,

00:14:11.520 --> 00:14:15.600
compel the Class of 2023 to mobilize

00:14:15.600 --> 00:14:18.783
its unique gifts for a world in need.

00:14:20.220 --> 00:14:23.340
And the liberal education
you've acquired at Yale

00:14:23.340 --> 00:14:26.373
has prepared you well to do so.

00:14:28.290 --> 00:14:31.530
Look closely though, and you will see

00:14:31.530 --> 00:14:34.560
that in a pithy mission
statement dedicated

00:14:34.560 --> 00:14:38.670
to articulating our
university's entire purpose,

00:14:38.670 --> 00:14:43.670
Yale puts equal emphasis on
where we hope to achieve it.

00:14:45.450 --> 00:14:50.310
Indeed, we devote ourselves
to improving the world.

00:14:50.310 --> 00:14:54.240
As we do that, we retain our focus

00:14:54.240 --> 00:14:56.940
on the interdependent community

00:14:56.940 --> 00:14:59.430
in which this work is advanced,

00:14:59.430 --> 00:15:03.213
ever attuned to our immediate
social surroundings.

00:15:05.040 --> 00:15:08.010
As Justice Sonia Sotomayor said

00:15:08.010 --> 00:15:11.767
to Yale Day of Service
participants some years ago,

00:15:11.767 --> 00:15:15.210
"Our tradition of civic work
is expressed in many ways

00:15:15.210 --> 00:15:19.800
including in city halls, in
state houses, in Congress,

00:15:19.800 --> 00:15:22.923
in the cabinet, and yes,
even on the Supreme Court.

00:15:23.940 --> 00:15:26.947
But this tradition of
service," she continued,

00:15:26.947 --> 00:15:29.860
"is also powerfully expressed daily

00:15:30.720 --> 00:15:35.220
by countless Yale
graduates, fired by a spirit

00:15:35.220 --> 00:15:40.220
of generosity, by relief
workers amidst disasters,

00:15:41.580 --> 00:15:46.380
mentors in urban schools,
volunteers in parks,

00:15:46.380 --> 00:15:50.487
libraries and museums,
and so many other roles."

00:15:51.990 --> 00:15:54.573
I think, for instance,
of a former student,

00:15:55.440 --> 00:15:58.470
Caroline Tanbee Smith,
whose love for this city

00:15:58.470 --> 00:16:02.880
was catalyzed by her president's
public service fellowship.

00:16:02.880 --> 00:16:04.770
Caroline stayed in New Haven

00:16:04.770 --> 00:16:07.440
after graduating from Yale College.

00:16:07.440 --> 00:16:10.560
And in 2017, she co-founded a nonprofit

00:16:10.560 --> 00:16:15.560
community accelerator dedicated
to making entrepreneurship

00:16:15.599 --> 00:16:18.990
more accessible,
particularly for low-income

00:16:18.990 --> 00:16:22.860
community members, women
and people of color,

00:16:22.860 --> 00:16:25.890
helping them to create
new jobs and generate

00:16:25.890 --> 00:16:29.343
millions of dollars in
revenue for this region.

00:16:31.627 --> 00:16:36.457
"Those efforts, your efforts,"
Justice Sotomayor said,

00:16:36.457 --> 00:16:38.940
"may not make headlines,

00:16:38.940 --> 00:16:42.237
but they most surely make a difference."

00:16:43.380 --> 00:16:47.550
Indeed, your efforts as
students engaged in service

00:16:47.550 --> 00:16:52.353
showcase why the where of Yale's
mission statement matters.

00:16:54.120 --> 00:16:59.120
Your efforts show that the
transformative is within reach,

00:17:02.040 --> 00:17:05.214
that there is marvelous
grandeur in the constellation

00:17:05.214 --> 00:17:10.214
of seemingly small acts of
service with and for each other

00:17:11.850 --> 00:17:16.320
that together constitute
a thriving civic life

00:17:16.320 --> 00:17:17.523
on this campus.

00:17:19.140 --> 00:17:21.780
The sum of your contributions has brought

00:17:21.780 --> 00:17:26.340
immense pride to Yale and
an equal measure of progress

00:17:26.340 --> 00:17:27.573
to our community.

00:17:29.460 --> 00:17:33.480
Here you have heeded Bob
Dylan and strapped yourself

00:17:33.480 --> 00:17:36.873
to a tree with roots and nourished them.

00:17:39.120 --> 00:17:42.180
Of course, we hope some
of you will consider

00:17:42.180 --> 00:17:47.180
his next refrain also,
"You ain't going nowhere,"

00:17:47.490 --> 00:17:50.613
and that you join us
right here in New Haven.

00:17:52.350 --> 00:17:55.410
But for those of you
who will soon disperse

00:17:55.410 --> 00:17:59.730
around the globe, also a fine outcome,

00:17:59.730 --> 00:18:04.730
we hope too that you will
reprise what you have done here.

00:18:06.030 --> 00:18:10.050
We hope that you'll be known
as much for your curiosity

00:18:10.050 --> 00:18:14.433
of mind as your generosity of spirit.

00:18:15.720 --> 00:18:20.720
We hope that wherever it is
upon life's sea you sail,

00:18:23.130 --> 00:18:28.130
you will arrive there,
not only as proud alumni

00:18:28.140 --> 00:18:33.140
of this university, but as
emissaries of its ethos,

00:18:34.650 --> 00:18:39.650
ready once again to raise
your hand and engage.

00:18:42.090 --> 00:18:46.344
Congratulations, Class of 2023.

00:18:46.344 --> 00:18:49.344
(audience applauds)

